---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Header from "../sections/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../sections/Footer.astro";
import "./privacy.css";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Mobile privacy policy | OpenDucktor"
  description="How OpenDucktor mobile handles host connections, local data, attachments, and Android QR scanner diagnostics."
  indexable={true}
>
  <Header />
  <main id="main" class="privacy wrap" tabindex="-1">
    <article>
      <header>
        <h1>OpenDucktor mobile privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="privacy-date">Last updated: <time datetime="2026-11-09">9 November 2026</time></p>
        <p>
          This policy covers the OpenDucktor app for iOS and Android. Maxime Ribera publishes the
          app. Contact <a class="text-link" href="mailto:contact@openducktor.dev"
            >contact@openducktor.dev</a
          > with questions about this policy or the app's handling of data.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-host">
        <h2 id="privacy-host">Your computer and your providers</h2>
        <p>
          The mobile app connects directly to the OpenDucktor computer that you add. We do not
          provide a hosted project service. You do not need an OpenDucktor cloud account.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app sends your instructions, task changes, permission responses, terminal input, and
          selected attachments to that computer. Your computer keeps the project files, tasks, and
          agent sessions. Your coding agent can send prompts, code, and attachments to the model
          provider that you set up. That provider's terms and privacy policy apply to its service.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-device">
        <h2 id="privacy-device">Data on your phone or tablet</h2>
        <p>
          The app saves the host addresses and names that you add. It also saves drafts, the
          selected workspace, and interface settings on your device. Pairing credentials use the
          operating system's secure storage. Files opened from a host can be stored temporarily on
          your device.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app uses the camera to scan a host pairing QR code. The app does not record audio.
          When you select a photo or file to attach, the app sends that item to the connected
          computer. It does not upload your photo library. Saving or sharing an item uses the
          operating system's controls and the destination that you choose.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your operating system may include app files in device backups, according to its backup
          settings.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-android">
        <h2 id="privacy-android">Android QR scanner diagnostics</h2>
        <p>
          The Android QR scanner uses Google ML Kit. Google's SDK collects device and app
          information, an installation identifier, performance measurements, scanner events, API
          settings, input and output sizes, and error codes for diagnostics and usage analytics.
          Google states that this data uses HTTPS in transit. These diagnostics are separate from
          the projects and messages sent to your OpenDucktor computer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read <a
            class="text-link"
            href="https://developers.google.com/ml-kit/android-data-disclosure"
            >Google's ML Kit data disclosure</a
          > and <a class="text-link" href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"
            >Google's privacy policy</a
          > for details about Google's handling of this data. OpenDucktor does not include ads or an advertising
          SDK.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-control">
        <h2 id="privacy-control">Connections, retention, and deletion</h2>
        <p>
          Use HTTPS or an encrypted private network to connect to a remote computer. The app also
          supports HTTP for hosts on a protected network. HTTP alone does not encrypt your traffic.
          A pairing code authorizes access; it does not encrypt the connection.
        </p>
        <p>
          Removing a saved host clears its pairing credentials and its local drafts and settings.
          This does not delete the projects or tasks on that computer. The computer's operator
          controls those records and can revoke a paired device's access. Model providers and Google
          control the retention of data that their services receive under their own policies.
        </p>
        <p>
          For questions or requests about data that the publisher holds, email <a
            class="text-link"
            href="mailto:contact@openducktor.dev">contact@openducktor.dev</a
          >. For records on a host or in a model provider's service, use that host's or provider's
          data controls.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-audience">
        <h2 id="privacy-audience">Audience and changes</h2>
        <p>
          OpenDucktor mobile is intended for adults aged 18 and over. We do not market it to
          children.
        </p>
        <p>
          We will update this page when the app's data practices change. The date above identifies
          the current policy.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</SiteLayout>
